Both platforms sell visibility, but not at the same moment of the decision. Choosing the wrong one means paying to be seen by people who aren't buying.

## The core difference: intent versus attention

On Google, people type what they want: “dentist rabat”, “air conditioning price casablanca”. They already have a need, and your ad arrives while they search. On Facebook and Instagram, people scroll their feed. They aren't looking for anything, and your visual has to stop them and make them want it.

## When Google Ads is the better choice

- Services people search for when the need arises: repairs, health, legal, rentals, training.
- Considered purchases where people compare before calling, in B2B as with consumers.
- Local searches such as “near me” or “[trade] + city”.
- Products searched by name or reference, through Google Shopping.

## When Facebook and Instagram ads are the better choice

- Products that sell on sight: fashion, cosmetics, decor, restaurants, real estate.
- Impulse buys and novelties nobody is searching for yet.
- Events, openings and time-limited promotions.
- Retargeting people who already visited your website without buying.

## Quick comparison

